2. Mathematical Framework

2.1 Observation Model and Joint Future

Let (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{P}) be a probability space. An admissible data-generating process is a joint distribution P over (G, S, X_{O,1:T}, Y_U) satisfying graph consistency, metadata admissibility, and temporal consistency.

Definition 2.1 (Observational Equivalence). Two processes P_1, P_2 \in \mathcal{P} are observationally equivalent if P_1(\mathcal{D}) = P_2(\mathcal{D}) for all measurable \mathcal{D}.

Definition 2.2 (Compatible Target Set). For a realized observation d,

\mathcal{Q}(d) = \left\{ P(Y_j \mid \mathcal{D} = d) : P \in \mathcal{P}, \, P(\mathcal{D} = d) > 0 \right\}.

Definition 2.3 (Identifiability). The target distribution is identifiable at d if |\mathcal{Q}(d)| = 1. It is partially identified if |\mathcal{Q}(d)| > 1 but the set is non-trivial.

2.2 ε-Invariant Transportability

Assumption A1 (Local Markov). For each target j \in U and time t,

X_{j,t+1} \perp\!\!\!\perp X_{V \setminus (N(j) \cup U)} \mid X_{N(j),t}, X_{U,t}, S_j.

Assumption A2 (ε-Invariant Mechanism). There exists a function f_\theta and a constant \varepsilon \geq 0 such that for all i \in O and j \in U with \text{type}(i) = \text{type}(j),

\| f_\theta(\cdot, S_j) - f_\theta(\cdot, S_i) \|_{\infty} \leq \varepsilon \cdot d_{\text{sem}}(S_j, S_i),

where d_{\text{sem}} is a bounded semantic distance on metadata. When \varepsilon = 0, A2 reduces to the strict invariance of v3.0.

Assumption A3 (Overlap). For every target metadata S_j,

n_{\text{obs}}(j) = |\{k \in O : d_{\text{geo}}(k, j) \leq d_{\max} \text{ and } \text{type}(k) = \text{type}(j)\}| \geq 1.

Falsification protocol for A2. A2 is falsified by placebo masking: mask a random subset O' \subset O during training, train f_\theta on O \setminus O', and test whether f_\theta predicts O''s future states with error exceeding a threshold \tau_\varepsilon. The estimate \hat{\varepsilon} is obtained as the maximum over held-out observed node pairs of the same type:

\hat{\varepsilon} = \max_{i, k \in O, \, \text{type}(i) = \text{type}(k)} \frac{\| f_\theta(X_{N(i),t}, S_i) - X_{i,t+1} - (f_\theta(X_{N(k),t}, S_k) - X_{k,t+1}) \|_{\infty}}{d_{\text{sem}}(S_i, S_k)}.

Theorem 2.4 (ε-Error Propagation). Under A1, A2 with parameter \varepsilon, and A3, the identified set width satisfies

W(d) \leq W_0(d) + C_\varepsilon \cdot \varepsilon \cdot H,

where W_0(d) is the width under exact invariance (\varepsilon = 0), C_\varepsilon is a constant depending on the Lipschitz constant of f_\theta and the noise variance, and H is the forecast horizon.

Proof sketch. The ε-perturbation of the transition function propagates through the recursive composition f_\theta \circ \cdots \circ f_\theta. Since f_\theta is Lipschitz with constant L_f, the perturbation at step t is bounded by L_f^{t-1} \varepsilon \cdot d_{\text{sem}}. Summing over the horizon H yields the bound with C_\varepsilon = \sum_{t=0}^{H-1} L_f^t. \square

Remark. This result makes the assumption refutable: if \hat{\varepsilon} estimated by placebo masking is large, the bound widens. The constant C_\varepsilon is computable from the trained model's Lipschitz constant and the horizon.

2.3 Shape-Constrained Process Class

Definition 2.5 (Shape-Constrained Process Class). Let \mathcal{P}_{\text{shape}} be the subset of \mathcal{P} satisfying:

1. Lipschitz continuity: |X_{j,t+1} - X_{i,t+1}| \leq L \cdot d_G(j, i) for all j, i \in V, t.
2. Dataset-specific conservation:
   · For speed-only datasets (METR-LA): bounded acceleration |X_{j,t+1} - X_{j,t}| \leq a_{\max}.
   · For flow datasets (PEMS-BAY flow): flow conservation \sum_{i \in \text{in}(j)} X_{i,t} = \sum_{k \in \text{out}(j)} X_{k,t+1} + \epsilon_{j,t} with \|\epsilon\| \leq \epsilon_{\max}.
   · For solar datasets (NREL-AL): bounded temporal variation |X_{j,t+1} - X_{j,t}| \leq \sigma_{\max}.
3. Fundamental diagram constraint (for speed datasets): X_{j,t} = v(\rho_{j,t}) where v(\cdot) is the speed-density fundamental diagram.
4. Wasserstein proximity: W_1(\mu_U, \mu_O) \leq \delta.
5. Support constraints: X_{j,t} \in [v_{\min}^{\text{type}(j)}, v_{\max}^{\text{type}(j)}].

The fundamental diagram constraint connects speed and density via the relation v = v_f(1 - \rho/\rho_{\text{jam}}) for the triangular fundamental diagram, where v_f is free-flow speed and \rho_{\text{jam}} is jam density. This is the correct constraint for speed datasets, replacing the physically invalid flow conservation.

2.4 Distance-Based Bound and Grounded Laplacian

Theorem 2.6 (Distance-Based Bound). Under the Lipschitz constraint and Wasserstein proximity, for any target j \in U,

W(d) \leq 2L \cdot d_G(j, O) + \delta,

where d_G(j, O) = \min_{i \in O} d_G(j, i) is the graph distance from j to the nearest observed node.

Proof. Let i^* = \arg\min_{i \in O} d_G(j, i). By Lipschitz continuity, for any two processes P_1, P_2 \in \mathcal{P}_{\text{shape}},

|E_{P_1}[g(Y_j)] - E_{P_2}[g(Y_j)]| \leq |E_{P_1}[g(Y_j)] - g(X_{i^*})| + |g(X_{i^*}) - E_{P_2}[g(Y_j)]|.

The first term is bounded by L \cdot d_G(j, i^*) by Lipschitz continuity. The second term is bounded by the same quantity. The Wasserstein proximity contributes an additional \delta. Summing yields the bound. \square

Proposition 2.7 (Grounded Laplacian Bound). Let L_g(O) be the grounded Laplacian obtained by removing rows and columns of the full graph Laplacian corresponding to observed nodes O. Then

W(d) \leq \frac{C \cdot \varepsilon_{\max}}{\sqrt{\lambda_{\min}(L_g(O))}} + L \cdot \text{diam}(G),

where \lambda_{\min}(L_g(O)) is the smallest eigenvalue of the grounded Laplacian.

Remark. This bound holds under the additional assumption of linear diffusion dynamics. Under congested (nonlinear) regimes, the distance-based bound of Theorem 2.6 is the provable baseline. The relationship between \lambda_{\min}(L_g(O)) and empirical bound width is tested as a hypothesis, not assumed as a theorem. The smallest eigenvalue of the grounded Laplacian is upper bounded by k for k grounded nodes , and bottlenecks among non-grounded nodes cause the smallest eigenvector component to approach zero , which directly captures the intuition that a target separated from observed nodes by a bottleneck has a wider identified set.

2.5 Calibration as a Diagnostic

Definition 2.8 (Calibration Error). For a predictive distribution Q and true outcome Y,

\mathrm{CalErr}(Q, Y) = \sup_{p \in [0,1]} \left| \mathbb{P}(Y \leq F_Q^{-1}(p)) - p \right|.

Definition 2.9 (Normalized Bound Width). W_{\text{norm}}(d) = W(d) / (y_{\max} - y_{\min}), so that W_{\text{norm}} \in [0, 1].

Hypothesis 2.10 (Calibration–Identifiability Monotonicity). For existing FUNS methods, across a population of target nodes \{j_1, \ldots, j_K\},

\rho_S(\mathrm{CalErr}, W_{\text{norm}}) > 0,

where \rho_S is Spearman's rank correlation. The hypothesis is falsified if \rho_S \leq 0 with statistical significance.

Theorem 2.11 (Impossibility of Distribution-Free Coverage at Unseen Nodes). Under the unrestricted process class \mathcal{P}, no conformal prediction procedure can achieve finite-sample coverage guarantee 1 - \alpha at unseen nodes.

Proof sketch. Conformal prediction requires exchangeability between calibration and test scores . In the inductive setting, message passing with new nodes causes an implicit shift in the calibration scores, breaking exchangeability . Specifically, as soon as a test node becomes part of the receptive field of any calibration node, the conformity scores are no longer exchangeable . Since traffic graphs are not node-exchangeable (spatial structure imposes dependence), the exchangeability assumption fails, and no distribution-free finite-sample guarantee exists. \square

Remark. This impossibility result directly supports the calibration degradation hypothesis: calibration error at unobserved nodes is not a failure of specific methods but a fundamental consequence of broken exchangeability.

2.6 Minimax Regret Value of Information with Randomization

Definition 2.12 (Randomized Policy). Let a: \mathcal{Z} \to \Delta(\mathcal{A}) be a randomized policy mapping prospective measurements Z_j to distributions over decisions, where \Delta(\mathcal{A}) is the simplex over \mathcal{A}.

Definition 2.13 (Minimax Regret). For a policy class \Pi,

R^* = \inf_{a(\cdot) \in \Pi} \sup_{P \in \mathcal{P}_{\text{shape}}} \mathbb{E}_P\left[ L(a(Z), Y_U) - L(a^*(P), Y_U) \right],

where a^*(P) = \arg\min_a \mathbb{E}_P[L(a, Y_U)] is the oracle action under P.

Theorem 2.14 (Randomization under Severe Partial Identification). If the identified set \mathcal{P}_{\text{shape}} is sufficiently wide, there exist infinitely many minimax-regret optimal policies, all of which sometimes randomize the policy recommendation.

Proof. This follows directly from Olea, Qiu, and Stoye (2026), who show that in a general class of problems with Gaussian likelihood, for severe enough partial identification, there are infinitely many minimax-regret optimal decision rules, all of which sometimes randomize . The randomization arises because the minimax regret criterion is linear in the policy, and the extreme points of the policy simplex correspond to deterministic rules, but the optimum may lie in the interior when the identified set is wide . \square

Definition 2.15 (Minimax Regret VoI). For prospective measurement Z_j at cost c_j,

\mathrm{VoI}(j) = R^*_{\text{current}} - \mathbb{E}_{Z_j \mid d}\left[ R^*_{\text{after } Z_j} \right] - c_j,

where R^*_{\text{current}} and R^*_{\text{after } Z_j} are defined as in Definition 2.13. The acquisition criterion is j^* = \arg\max_{j \in C} \mathrm{VoI}(j).

Proposition 2.16 (Divergence from Mutual Information). There exists a graph G, loss function L, and identified set \mathcal{P}_{\text{shape}} such that the sensor selected by mutual information differs from the sensor selected by minimax regret VoI.

Counterexample. Consider a line graph 1 - 2 - 3 - 4 with O = \{1, 2\} and target j = 4. Candidate sensor locations are \{3, 5\} where node 5 is a new location adjacent to node 4. Mutual information I(X_3; Y_4) is maximal at node 3 due to proximity. However, if the loss function is asymmetric with c_1 \gg c_2 (underestimation costlier), and the identified set is wide, the minimax regret VoI selects node 5 because it resolves uncertainty about the worst-case outcome at node 4, which node 3 does not. The divergence occurs because mutual information maximizes expected reduction in entropy (symmetric) , while minimax regret minimizes worst-case loss (asymmetric). \square

Remark on submodularity. The (1 - 1/e) submodularity guarantee of Krause et al. does not transfer to minimax regret VoI because the objective is not submodular. The greedy algorithm is a heuristic; a guarantee is proven only for the special case of binary decisions with a single threshold.

