Appendix B: Detailed Proofs

B.1 Proof of Theorem 2.4 (ε-Error Propagation)

Theorem 2.4 (ε-Error Propagation). Under A1, A2 with parameter \varepsilon, and A3, the identified set width satisfies

W(d) \leq W_0(d) + C_\varepsilon \cdot \varepsilon \cdot H,

where W_0(d) is the width under exact invariance (\varepsilon = 0), C_\varepsilon = \sum_{t=0}^{H-1} L_f^t, and L_f is the Lipschitz constant of f_\theta.

Proof. Let P_1, P_2 \in \mathcal{P}_{\text{shape}} be two processes consistent with the observation \mathcal{D} = d. Under A2 with parameter \varepsilon, for any observed node i \in O and unobserved node j \in U with \text{type}(i) = \text{type}(j),

\| f_\theta(\cdot, S_j) - f_\theta(\cdot, S_i) \|_{\infty} \leq \varepsilon \cdot d_{\text{sem}}(S_j, S_i).

Consider the recursive composition of the transition function over horizon H. Let f^{(t)} denote the t-fold composition. By Lipschitz continuity of f_\theta with constant L_f,

\| f^{(t)}(\cdot, S_j) - f^{(t)}(\cdot, S_i) \|_{\infty} \leq L_f^t \cdot \varepsilon \cdot d_{\text{sem}}(S_j, S_i).

The difference in the expected target functional between P_1 and P_2 is bounded by

| \mathbb{E}_{P_1}[g(Y_j)] - \mathbb{E}_{P_2}[g(Y_j)] | \leq \sum_{t=0}^{H-1} \| g \|_{\text{Lip}} \cdot L_f^t \cdot \varepsilon \cdot d_{\text{sem}}(S_j, S_i).

Taking the supremum over P_1, P_2 and using the boundedness of d_{\text{sem}} yields the result with C_\varepsilon = \| g \|_{\text{Lip}} \cdot \sum_{t=0}^{H-1} L_f^t. \square

Remark B.1.1. The constant C_\varepsilon grows exponentially in H when L_f > 1, reflecting the compounding of invariance violations over long horizons. For stable dynamics (L_f < 1), C_\varepsilon converges to \| g \|_{\text{Lip}} / (1 - L_f).

