B.2 Proof of Theorem 2.6 (Distance-Based Bound)

Theorem 2.6 (Distance-Based Bound). Under the Lipschitz constraint and Wasserstein proximity, for any target j \in U,

W(d) \leq 2L \cdot d_G(j, O) + \delta.

Proof. Let i^* = \arg\min_{i \in O} d_G(j, i) be the nearest observed node to j. By the Lipschitz constraint, for any process P \in \mathcal{P}_{\text{shape}},

| \mathbb{E}_P[g(Y_j)] - g(X_{i^*}) | \leq L \cdot d_G(j, i^*).

For any two processes P_1, P_2 \in \mathcal{P}_{\text{shape}},

| \mathbb{E}_{P_1}[g(Y_j)] - \mathbb{E}_{P_2}[g(Y_j)] | \leq | \mathbb{E}_{P_1}[g(Y_j)] - g(X_{i^*}) | + | g(X_{i^*}) - \mathbb{E}_{P_2}[g(Y_j)] |.

Each term is bounded by L \cdot d_G(j, i^*), giving

| \mathbb{E}_{P_1}[g(Y_j)] - \mathbb{E}_{P_2}[g(Y_j)] | \leq 2L \cdot d_G(j, i^*).

The Wasserstein proximity constraint W_1(\mu_U, \mu_O) \leq \delta contributes an additional additive term \delta, because the optimal coupling between \mu_U and \mu_O can shift the expected functional by at most \delta in Wasserstein-1 distance. Therefore,

W(d) = \sup_{P_1, P_2} | \mathbb{E}_{P_1}[g(Y_j)] - \mathbb{E}_{P_2}[g(Y_j)] | \leq 2L \cdot d_G(j, O) + \delta. \quad \square

Remark B.2.1. The bound is tight when the target is connected to the observed set by a single path, and the Lipschitz constraint is active along that path. For a target adjacent to an observed node (d_G(j, O) = 1), W(d) \leq 2L + \delta.

Remark B.2.2. The distance-based bound does not require spectral graph theory. It is provable from Lipschitz continuity alone and serves as the provable baseline for all subsequent spectral refinements.

