B.3 Proof of Proposition 2.7 (Grounded Laplacian Bound)

Proposition 2.7 (Grounded Laplacian Bound). Let L_g(O) be the grounded Laplacian obtained by removing rows and columns of the full graph Laplacian corresponding to observed nodes O. Under linear diffusion dynamics, the bound width satisfies

W(d) \leq \frac{C \cdot \varepsilon_{\max}}{\sqrt{\lambda_{\min}(L_g(O))}} + L \cdot \text{diam}(G).

Proof sketch. The grounded Laplacian L_g(O) governs the convergence rate of diffusion processes from unobserved nodes to the observed boundary. The smallest eigenvalue \lambda_{\min}(L_g(O)) is upper bounded by |O|, with equality if and only if all grounded nodes connect to all other nodes . For a given subset S \subset V of grounded nodes, \lambda_{\min}(L_g(S)) is bounded below by graph-theoretic quantities involving bottlenecks .

Under linear diffusion dynamics, the steady-state variance at unobserved nodes is proportional to \varepsilon_{\max}^2 / \lambda_{\min}(L_g(O)). The Lipschitz constraint propagates this variance to the target functional, yielding the bound. The diameter term accounts for the maximum propagation distance. \square

Remark B.3.1. The bound holds under linear diffusion dynamics. Under congested (nonlinear) regimes, the distance-based bound of Theorem 2.6 is the provable baseline. The relationship between \lambda_{\min}(L_g(O)) and empirical bound width is tested as a hypothesis in World C of the synthetic benchmark.

