B.4 Proof of Theorem 2.11 (Impossibility of Distribution-Free Coverage)

Theorem 2.11 (Impossibility of Distribution-Free Coverage at Unseen Nodes). Under the unrestricted process class \mathcal{P}, no conformal prediction procedure can achieve finite-sample coverage guarantee 1 - \alpha at unseen nodes.

Proof. Conformal prediction requires exchangeability between calibration and test scores to obtain a valid distribution-free coverage guarantee . In the inductive setting, the graph is progressively expanded by nodes and edges introduced over time, and test nodes are not present at the training and calibration stage. For GNNs, updates in the graph cause an implicit shift in the embeddings of existing nodes and consequently in the conformity scores. Therefore, even assuming node/edge exchangeability, the coverage guarantee is no longer valid. Intuitively, calibration scores are no longer exchangeable with test scores as soon as new nodes or edges are introduced .

Specifically, as soon as a test node becomes part of the receptive field of any calibration node, the conformity scores are no longer exchangeable . Since traffic graphs are not node-exchangeable (spatial structure imposes dependence), the exchangeability assumption fails, and no distribution-free finite-sample guarantee exists for unseen nodes under \mathcal{P}. \square

Remark B.4.1. This impossibility result directly supports the calibration degradation hypothesis: calibration error at unobserved nodes is not a failure of specific methods but a fundamental consequence of broken exchangeability. The result strengthens the claim that identifiability is a prerequisite for reliable uncertainty quantification.

