B.5 Proof of Theorem 2.14 (Randomization under Severe Partial Identification)

Theorem 2.14 (Randomization under Severe Partial Identification). If the identified set \mathcal{P}_{\text{shape}} is sufficiently wide, there exist infinitely many minimax-regret optimal policies, all of which sometimes randomize the policy recommendation.

Proof. This follows directly from Olea, Qiu, and Stoye (2026), who show that in a general class of problems with Gaussian likelihood: (i) all decision rules are admissible; (ii) it is maximin-welfare optimal to ignore all data; and (iii) for severe enough partial identification, there are infinitely many minimax-regret optimal decision rules, all of which sometimes randomize the policy recommendation . The randomization arises because the minimax regret criterion is linear in the policy, and the extreme points of the policy simplex correspond to deterministic rules, but the optimum may lie in the interior when the identified set is wide. They uniquely characterize the minimax-regret optimal rule that least frequently randomizes and show that, in some cases, it can outperform other minimax-regret optimal rules in terms of profiled regret . \square

Remark B.5.1. This result has a direct implication for sensor placement: the acquisition criterion should return a randomized policy when the identified set is wide. The randomization is not a computational artifact but a decision-theoretic necessity.

