Appendix G: Linear-Gaussian Toy Model

G.1 Model Specification

Let X_t \in \mathbb{R}^{|V|} follow a linear Gaussian state-space model:

X_{t+1} = A X_t + \varepsilon_t, \quad \varepsilon_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma),

where A is the state transition matrix and \Sigma is the noise covariance.

G.2 Closed-Form Posterior Predictive

With observed nodes O and unobserved nodes U, partition:

X_t = \begin{pmatrix} X_{O,t} \\ X_{U,t} \end{pmatrix}, \quad
A = \begin{pmatrix} A_{OO} & A_{OU} \\ A_{UO} & A_{UU} \end{pmatrix}, \quad
\Sigma = \begin{pmatrix} \Sigma_{OO} & \Sigma_{OU} \\ \Sigma_{UO} & \Sigma_{UU} \end{pmatrix}.

The posterior predictive p(Y_U \mid X_{O,1:T}) is Gaussian with closed-form mean and covariance. The identified set under Lipschitz and Wasserstein constraints can be computed exactly.

G.3 Validation Protocol

1. Set |V| = 50, |O| = 40, |U| = 10.
2. Generate synthetic trajectories with known A, \Sigma.
3. Compute sharp bounds analytically.
4. Compute bounds via discretized LP with Sinkhorn.
5. Compare analytical and numerical bounds.
6. Validate calibration diagnostic on synthetic targets.

